cause you know where they stand  or judge Ramon's so Dini  when he saw court personnel in the back  of his courtroom who appeared to be  bickering over splitting up payoffs that  they'd received from an attorney he told  his bailiff go tell those guys if  they're talking about money to go in the  back room and do it not to talk about it  you know out here in the open well all  of those kinds of things came out actual  testimony in trials but they weren't  something that we could have observed  his journalists or any other person who  just comes into the court could see now  you could see a lot of things that were  going on but Jim Tory and I have been  covering the courts in Cook County  off and on for more than 20 years and if  you had asked me to come testify before  a grand jury I wouldn't have a single  incident that I could ever have related  which is why it takes a massive federal  investigation of this sort to root it  out where the victims and stories like  this well of course we the citizens are  the ultimate victims if they're if the  justice system is corrupted we're  victims in a number of ways obviously if  the drunk driver isn't fined or  something we're financially hurt but  that's a minor part of it I think that  it you know it breeds disrespect for the  law that in it creates more lawlessness  than we would otherwise have and the  Cook County Circuit Court had the  reputation of being corrupt and has the  reputation today of being corrupt and I  think that's the real  damage that's done by these appearances  that justice is not done or that a case  can be fixed some judges can be wired  and another aspect of this corruption  just because of the reputation of the  system the average person who was  charged with a misdemeanor believed that  the system was corrupt so if a lawyer  said to him well my fee will be $500 and  then comes back right before the trial  or usually a bench trial and says well  we're gonna have to pay off the judge  it'll be another 250 it was a way that  it just enabled lawyers to extort money  in that way that was called rainmaking  by the people who engaged in it that is  telling the client that the lawyer was  going to pay a bribe but actually of  course just pocketing the money and it  was possible simply because of the  reputation of the system  interestingly lawyers could tell I mean  of course because of the the practice  that this is the evidence maybe a  witness is not going to show up or the  evidence is very weak  the defendants going to be acquitted for  sure the lawyer would know and so you  could do this with a great deal of  certainty but in a case where the lawyer  was surprised if the judge heard the  evidence and said no I find the  defendant guilty the lawyer would then  go back and say she who have you upset  in the prosecutor's office there was  tremendous heat in this case and the  judge just couldn't do it here's your  money back  in most cases were we talking about  large sums of money when some cases we  were talking about oh five hundred a  thousand dollars but in most cases and  in some cases we were talking about  extremely small amounts of money I think  one of the things that amazed observers  in Chicago was not that the corruption  was so rife but that justice was being  sold for so little some in some cases as  little as ten dollars a case  early on in your book he tells story  struck me of one particular judge who  occasionally didn't want to come to  court oh yes we tell it well that's  judge sadena who ran a he presided over  a gambling court at Central Police  Headquarters and early in the morning he  had to handle what's called the bum call  this is the call of all of the people  who were swept off the streets basically  for safekeeping  on any given night in Chicago they're  all brought in well judge Cedeno who  drank a lot would sometimes have a  hangover in the morning and he'd call in  and tell his bailiff to tell the police  sergeant assigned to his